  {
    "episode_id": 95941,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95941.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "You know, Grant, I think that you're exactly right and that the divisiveness does wrap up the closer we get to a presidential election, especially. And I do think that after the election, things will begin to settle back down. But what's more important is that I don't think it's okay. I think that in this country over the last several years, you have seen this migration toward increased divisiveness a much bigger gap between sort of the liberals and conservatives, this middle ground area is becoming more vacant. And folks are more strongly, at least in political circles, are more strongly associating themselves with a liberal or conservative ideology. Now the interesting thing is that the largest growth of any political party per se is actually independence. You're seeing more and more people that are associating themselves with political independence than you're seeing affiliated with a party. And so one Let me say it again. I think it's awful. What's happening too. I think it is contrary to the intents of our founders. I think it dilutes the whole concept behind a democracy, this constitutional republic. and it really infringes upon our ability to represent people like you, the people that we represent because more of the power is being amassed within the political parties and the leadership and not within the individual members and of course the people that we're representing like you. And so I think it's dangerous, I think it's a bad trajectory, I think it undermines our awesome structure of government and it seems that the people are largely moving a direction of asking for more cooperation and more focus on what the actual right thing to do is rather than the right thing for a political party.",
