    "turn_text": "from the United States. and turned around and effectively used it against this economically or labor-wise or competing or undercutting US businesses, it's a real issue. And the fact that you have one drone manufacturer that has well in excess of the majority of the industry, it did raise concerns. And look, it's guilt by affiliation. But it did raise concerns because of some of the theft of intellectual property. And I think people view this as one of those high-tech spaces where you're slapping different sensors and cameras and technologies onto drones, and that if this has a manufacturer that is associated with China, just like you've seen with the 5G issue, it definitely is raising additional concerns in the United States. But the good news is, and I think you kind of alluded to this, Just in the last few months, we have already seen this migration of drone technology and manufacturers in the United States of growing presence, a larger percentage of drones being U.S. manufactured and some of our allies as well. And so I do think that there are going to be options or solutions there. I think having any one drone manufacturer that has 85 to 90% of the drones is probably not the best idea, like competition like you do. And so I think that this issue is going to be resolved. But let me just reiterate, Grant, this sensitivity toward China is a real issue, and I think that it is going to continue to grow.",
