  {
    "episode_id": 95941,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95941.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, the job in Congress, I do enjoy it, you know, just without rehashing the entire dialogue. I could do without some of the partisan fighting. I really do enjoy the policy. I enjoy finding solutions to these longstanding problems. And you and I, last time we spoke, just briefly covered some of the things that we've been able to get done here that have been Stuck or been problems since the honestly before I was born the seventies and in some cases eighties and nineties and and so I look I love that I love Trying to find solutions to complex problems working with diverse interests to try and figure out where we can get to that middle ground and getting things done. But the divisiveness is really a frustrating environment to work on. And you have to work a lot harder to simply do what is right than you should in Congress. And that's unfortunate. Specifically in the aviation subcommittee, Grant, I was excited about the opportunity to lead the aviation subcommittee because I've done a lot of work in other areas of infrastructure. in highways in South Louisiana, restoring our coastal estuaries, providing hurricane and flood protection. And as you and I both know, whether you want to talk about traffic solutions or you want to talk about flood and coastal resiliency solutions, the projects that we're carrying out today are often projects, as I mentioned, that we're conceived back in the 70s, 80s, and 90s. One of the things about aviation that's exciting in aviation infrastructure is that it's that one sector that's so technologically advanced that largely we can be thinking about what's right for the 2030s, 2040s, and 2050s. And that was one of the things that really attracted me to this topic. And so going into it, I was really excited about truly being able to plan for, think about the future, ensure the US continued to maintain this technological edge. But as you know, Within months, the 737 MAX disasters came about. And so that has really and appropriately kind of caused the priorities to be associated with getting this issue resolved, whether it's the certification process, whether it's manufacturing, but just getting this safety issue resolved. And so many of the things that we were excited to be working on had to and I want to say again appropriately be put on a back burner to where we can address this but but look you know you've still still seen you know remote ID you've still seen a number of other advances in the integration of drones into our complex aviation space. And so while certainly not the experience that I was anticipating early on, nonetheless, this has been a rewarding and challenging assignment that I have enjoyed working on. And I'm going to continue to enjoy working on. I do want to get the 737 MAX issue resolved in a way that's safe and addresses the concerns of the victims' families and restores confidence aviation system, but also ensuring we do not lose ground in the technological edge and the opportunities that drones are providing in the United States today and well in the future.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
